 "how_to_read_these_numbers": [
  "These are a POLICY's performance on a simulated supply base of 52 plants, not the risk model's performance. The model's own metrics are in early_warning_summary_h*.json, measured on the full establishment panel. A policy evaluated on 52 high-volume suppliers will not have the same error rates as the model has across 578 establishments.",
  "The rule-based baseline uses the last posted FSIS category regardless of how old it is, because that is exactly what the status quo does: FSIS categories persist between quarterly postings and a food bank looks up the current one. The score policies, by contrast, treat a score older than the staleness limit as no score at all. The two are not held to the same standard on purpose.",
  "'exposure' is pounds or meals distributed uncooked from plants whose sampling later deteriorated. It is NOT a count of illnesses, and the FSIS licence forbids attributing illness to an establishment. Nothing here does.",
  "Food loss arises from two mechanical causes only, both derived from the simulated shelf life: a review hold that leaves too little shelf life to distribute, and diverted pounds beyond the cooked-meal channel's capacity. No spoilage rate has been assumed."
 ],
 "guardrails": [
  "no output states that any food, lot or establishment is safe or unsafe",
  "no action is executed automatically; hold and divert require human confirmation",
  "no establishment is linked to any illness or outbreak anywhere in this script",
  "every pounds-denominated figure is simulated and flagged as such"
 ]
}